---
description: Rules for anything touching CleanupQueue, deletion, or the excluded-paths list
globs: ["Sources/DiskMapCore/CleanupQueue.swift", "Sources/DiskMapCore/AppLeftoverFinder.swift"]
alwaysApply: false
---

This code decides what a user's data cleanup tool is allowed to touch. Bugs
here have real, irreversible-feeling consequences even though everything
technically goes through the Trash.

- Staging (adding to `CleanupQueue`) must never be the same action as
  committing (moving to Trash). Keep that two-step boundary explicit in any
  new feature — a "scan and clean" one-click action is a product decision
  for a human to make deliberately, not something to default into while
  implementing something else.
- Any new source of staged items (a new scan type, a new heuristic) must go
  through `CleanupQueue.stage()`, which already enforces the excluded-paths
  check. Don't write a parallel path that skips it.
- `AppLeftoverFinder`'s matching is heuristic by design (no Apple API gives
  you "everything this app touched"). When improving matching accuracy,
  bias toward fewer false positives over fewer false negatives — a leftover
  DiskMap misses costs the user a few KB; a real file it wrongly flags
  costs the user's trust the first time they lose something that mattered.
- If you add a new "Quick Wins" style regenerable-directory pattern (e.g.
  matching `node_modules`, `.venv`), it should be data (a JSON list, per
  `docs/PRD.md`'s "beyond DiskBuddy" section) rather than hardcoded, so
  users and contributors can extend it without a code change.
